    "likely biasing this specific ranking toward better-documented European and South \n",
    "American players in the modern era.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 4. Team Efficiency\n",
    "Ranking teams by raw goal totals is misleading, as it rewards longevity over quality. \n",
    "After applying a minimum threshold of 100 matches played and filtering to FIFA member \n",
    "nations only, Brazil emerges as the most efficient team historically (2.11 points per \n",
    "match, 2.18 goals per match), followed by Spain, England, Germany, and — notably — \n",
    "Iran, which ranks 5th despite receiving less international media attention than \n",
    "traditional European/South American powers.\n",
    "\n",
    "Before filtering, the same analysis surfaced two non-FIFA entities (Jersey, Guernsey) \n",
    "in the top 10 — a clear illustration of why domain-aware filtering matters more than \n",
    "relying on raw statistical thresholds alone.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 5. Drama Analysis: Penalty Shootouts\n",
    "Penalty shootouts show a strong, near-continuous upward trend over time: from 0.1 \n",
    "shootouts/year in the 1970s to 17.7/year in the 2020s (a 170x increase). This does not \n",
    "appear to be a single \"breakthrough\" decade but a sustained trend, likely linked to the \n",
    "growing number of knockout-format tournaments and qualifiers worldwide.\n",
    "\n",
    "By tournament, smaller regional/alternative competitions like COSAFA Cup (17.8%) and \n",
    "the CONIFA World Football Cup (16.8%) show the highest shootout rates — driven largely \n",
    "by their knockout-heavy formats and, in COSAFA's case, rules that resolve even group-\n",
    "stage draws via shootouts, rather than by their popularity or prestige.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 6. Worst Performers: Scoring First, Failing to Win\n",
    "Across all 49,518 played matches, teams that scored first went on to win 75.7% of the \n",
    "time. Among teams with at least 30 instances of scoring first, Malta shows the highest \n",
    "failure rate (63.9%), followed by Luxembourg (48%), Venezuela (47.1%), and Kazakhstan \n",
    "(46.3%). These are largely smaller/mid-tier footballing nations, suggesting this \n",
    "pattern reflects a genuine gap in overall team strength against stronger opponents \n",
    "rather than a specific psychological \"collapse\" tendency.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 7. Era Comparison\n",
    "Three consistent trends emerge across 150 years of football:\n",
    "- **Goals per match** have roughly halved, from ~5.6 in the 1880s to ~2.7–2.8 in the \n",
    "  modern era (2000s onward), confirming the game has become more defensively organized.\n",
    "- **Draw rates** rose from ~15% in the 19th century to a peak of ~26% in the 1980s, \n",
    "  settling around 23% in recent decades.\n",
    "- **Average goal margin** has steadily declined from ~3.8 goals in the 1880s to ~1.6 \n",
    "  today, indicating matches have become far more competitively balanced.\n",
    "\n",
    "The heatmap of goals per match by decade and tournament confirms this is a broad, \n",
    "tournament-independent trend rather than one driven by a single competition.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Caveat:** The earliest decades (1870s–1880s) are based on very few matches (13–55) \n",
    "and were excluded from trend visualizations to avoid statistically unreliable \n",
    "comparisons.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 8. Outlier Detection\n",
    "The most lopsided match in recorded history is Australia 31–0 American Samoa (2001 \n",
    "World Cup qualifying) — a real, well-documented event, not a data error.\n",
    "\n",
    "Two outlier-detection methods were compared:\n",
    "- **IQR method** flagged 12.16% of all matches as outliers — an impractically high \n",
    "  share, driven by the naturally right-skewed distribution of goal margins (margins \n",
    "  cannot be negative, so extreme values pull the distribution's tail).\n",
    "- **Z-score method** (|Z| > 3) flagged a more reasonable 1.30% of matches, though the \n",
    "  distribution's skewness (2.56) means even this method's underlying normality \n",
    "  assumption is not fully met.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Conclusion:** Purely statistical thresholds are insufficient for this type of data. \n",
    "The most meaningful outliers were identified through a combination of statistical \n",
    "screening and contextual/domain knowledge (e.g., recognizing recognized historical \n",
    "records vs. mismatches against non-FIFA entities).\n",
    "\n",
    "## 9. Key Insights & Recommendations\n",
    "1. **For coaching staffs:** Brazil, Spain, and Iran stand out as the most efficient \n",
    "   teams historically (points/goals per match) — worth benchmarking tactical \n",
    "   philosophies against, beyond just the \"big names.\"\n",
    "2. **For federation strategists:** The steep, sustained rise in shootout frequency \n",
    "   suggests investing in dedicated penalty-taking training and psychological \n",
    "   preparation is increasingly high-value, not a minor afterthought.\n",
    "3. **For media analysts:** The \"narrative\" that certain teams are prone to \n",
    "   psychological collapses after taking an early lead (e.g., Malta, Luxembourg) is \n",
    "   better explained by an overall talent/strength gap against stronger opponents than \n",
    "   by any special mental fragility.\n",
    "4. Football has structurally become a lower-scoring, more balanced sport over 150 \n",
